s\n   \"`AGENTS.md` states the rule this enforces — never record an artifact at\n   `.`\", and AGENTS.md:30-34 describes the migration without stating that\n   rule — but that was already so on main, and is not this head's doing. A\n   one-line fix to :9 in a follow-up is enough; I am not holding the head for\n   a note file.\n\n2. SKILL.md:125-126 has the architecture conclusion name\n   `docs/reference/architecture.md`, a path that exists only in this\n   repository, while architecture.md:2775 now frames the three conclusions as\n   \"how any workroom reviews an implementation\". The coupling is pre-existing\n   — main's SKILL.md said the same — and the repair sharpens the framing\n   without creating it. Worth a thought if SKILL.md is ever copied into\n   another workroom, as docs/getting-started.md:269 and :296 describe.\n\nThe four points I raised as non-blocking are taken and correct. `gs status` is\nnamed and linked at SKILL.md:48-51, so the field at :190-191 belongs to a\ncommand the page names; that field is now written\n`durable.vocabulary.definitions`, which is the real path under the /v0/status\nbody root. The four act commands are linked at SKILL.md:32-35 and each names\n`--no-preflight`; I confirmed the flag is registered on all four in cmd/gs\n(main.go:559 state, :1832 ratify, :1880 supersede, :2130 batch) and documented\non each linked page. \"Or through supersession\" is restored at SKILL.md:173.\nLeaving the two bare directory links is a fair call now that the act pages are\nlinked directly — that was the reason I raised them.\n\n## Conditions\n\n1. Met. Every command the page names is registered in cmd/gs at this head,\n   including `status` (main.go:150, :2626) and the four act commands. All\n   twenty-one document links resolve — I extracted and resolved every one —\n   and the three anchors match headings in their targets. The five statements\n   I struck are corrected, and I re-derived each rather than accepting the\n   correction: presence states at host/live/live.go:45-48 and the switch at\n   :718-724, MaxFocusEvents at :50 enforced at :728, MCP advertisement at\n   cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:754-756, internal/service/server.go:676 the\n   focus-limit message alone; review.md:148-165 with head news at :34;\n   land.md:84-99; the flare diagnosis per internal/docset/docset.go:317-321\n   and :346-347 against internal/workroom/fold.go:3226-3233; and\n   DocsDir = \"docs\" at internal/docset/docset.go:20. Every one holds.\n\n2. Met, and I looked at this closely because the margin is thin. SKILL.md is\n   212 lines and 1466 words against main's 213 and 1499 — I measured all four.\n   The condition sets a threshold, not a target, and it is cleared on both\n   counts. The clause that matters more is \"no step losing a rule an actor\n   must follow\", and that is satisfied better than at the head I first\n   reviewed: the three rules I found orphaned or dropped are back, and every\n   removed refusal list is covered in a page SKILL.md now links —\n   promise.md:63-69, artifact.md:79-92 with the changed-path warning at :106,\n   review-request.md:66-73, review.md:148-165 and :34, land.md:84-99, and\n   merge.md:586 for the retirements a merge may make. The pair an actor reads\n   each cycle went from 2255 words to 1741, a 23% cut. Four lines spent\n   restoring correctness is a good trade, not a regression.\n\n3. Met. The drift is resolved towards SKILL.md and docs/reference/architecture.md\n   now records that as the contract, in this head, with its artifact published.\n\n4. Met, run by me at exactly d4726958 in the clean checkout:\n   `go test -count=1 -timeout 40m ./internal/docset/...` ok 39.715s, exit 0,\n   and `go test -timeout 40m ./...` exit 0, 36 packages, no failures. One\n   qualification: in my run the standalone docset run came first, so docset\n   was cached inside the full suite. I therefore confirm both exit codes and\n   the uncached docset timing, but not the artifact's in-suite 40.0s or the\n  